WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.125 --> 00:00:03.461
I grew up with
the notion that anything was possible.

00:00:04.254 --> 00:00:09.592
My dad was a firm believer
in aspiring to the best of your ability

00:00:09.676 --> 00:00:11.970
and even unimaginable realities.

00:00:17.684 --> 00:00:19.728
The story of Hello Tomorrow

00:00:19.936 --> 00:00:22.230
is so personal in so many ways.

00:00:22.689 --> 00:00:25.275
It's an imagined world
that's talking about

00:00:25.734 --> 00:00:28.486
the American aspirational dream.

00:00:29.362 --> 00:00:31.781
My dad was a salesman.

00:00:32.866 --> 00:00:35.493
I grew up in a house where the closet

00:00:35.493 --> 00:00:38.747
was filled with products
that were floating through my childhood.

00:00:39.497 --> 00:00:42.167
Inflatable ice chests, hat umbrellas.

00:00:42.917 --> 00:00:45.170
None of which ever had any success.

00:00:45.837 --> 00:00:48.673
But every single one of them
was going to be the key to our future.

00:00:51.301 --> 00:00:53.386
One of the things that you try to do
as an actor

00:00:53.553 --> 00:00:56.389
is use somebody close to you

00:00:56.556 --> 00:01:00.310
as a reference point for a character. To try to get inside

00:01:00.310 --> 00:01:02.020
the head of your father
has been fraught in so many ways.

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:04.439
the head of your father
has been fraught in so many ways.

00:01:05.315 --> 00:01:08.151
When I think back about my father
now, always like, keep your chin up.

00:01:08.693 --> 00:01:10.570
It was because there was repeated
rejection.

00:01:10.945 --> 00:01:13.323
It's a desperate position to be in.

00:01:14.741 --> 00:01:16.701
We live with miracles at our are fingertips.

00:01:16.701 --> 00:01:19.204
We fly with the stars, we split atoms,

00:01:19.204 --> 00:01:22.248
we've got robots
taking out the trash. Why

00:01:22.248 --> 00:01:27.045
are we all still waiting to live our dreams?

00:01:29.881 --> 00:01:32.967
Like all people,
we go through periods of our life

00:01:32.967 --> 00:01:35.762
where hope is,
you know, sand in the palm of your hand.

00:01:36.805 --> 00:01:40.642
There is a great veneer
over these gadgets.

00:01:41.226 --> 00:01:44.104
What they're really doing
is giving somebody an opportunity

00:01:44.104 --> 00:01:46.523
to hope for a better future.

00:01:47.023 --> 00:01:48.942
And my dad never gave up on that.

00:01:49.567 --> 00:01:52.112
There's something so romantic
about that sense

00:01:52.112 --> 00:01:54.906
of unbridled, unabashed hopefulness.

00:01:55.365 --> 00:01:58.368
I've had some incredibly fortunate
opportunities in my career,

00:01:58.368 --> 00:02:00.328
and this certainly is up there with them.

00:02:04.582 --> 00:02:06.417
You get to dream on a better tomorrow,

00:02:08.503 --> 00:02:09.587
where you can have it all.

